<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-delphi10000-asterias-rubens-turing-wavelength-s0> a pk:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Asterias rubens Turing wavelength at 115.2 µm (Holocene, Pass 1 square patch) (Asterias rubens (common starfish), Holocene, 0 Ma)",
        "Asterias rubens Turing wavelength at 115.2 µm (Holocene, Pass 1 square patch) faithfully reproduces Asterias rubens (common starfish), Holocene, 0 Ma morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-asterias-rubens-turing-wavelength-s0-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-asterias-rubens-turing-wavelength-s0-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-asterias-rubens-turing-wavelength-s0-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-asterias-rubens-turing-wavelength-s0-4> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) models/2026-delphi10000-asterias-rubens-turing-wavelength-s0.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Asterias rubens (common starfish), Holocene, 0 Ma specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-asterias-rubens-turing-wavelength-s0.py> ;
    schema:about "Asterias rubens (common starfish), Holocene, 0 Ma" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: A second, independent assessor.",
        "does_not_claim: That 115.2 µm was measured as an ocellus diameter in Asterias rubens.",
        "does_not_claim: That Schnakenberg rate constants were identified in starfish epidermis.",
        "does_not_claim: That the studio photograph is a micrograph at this scale, or that its pixel wavelength is 115.2 µm.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: FTCS Neumann integration, radial-FFT wavelength, diffusivity sweep λ ∝ √s, equal-diffusion ablation.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Segmentation of the Hillewaert photograph (circularity, coherency, tubercle spacing in units of arm radius).",
        "reconstructed_in_script: a=0.072, b=0.918, D_v/D_u=7.62, domain 576.0 µm, seed 3123, chosen so λ_c = 115.2 µm and only the p=5 mode is unstable.",
        "reported_in_source: Garm & Nilsson 2014: most starfish have a compound eye at the arm tip; their navigation experiments used Linckia laevigata.",
        "reported_in_source: Murray 1981 writes the pre-pattern wavenumber used for animal markings.",
        "reported_in_source: Schnakenberg 1979 two-species reaction with a homogeneous steady state.",
        "reported_in_source: Turing 1952 diffusion-driven instability and a critical wavenumber.",
        "transfer_boundary: Coat-marking reaction-diffusion is transferred to an asteroid pigment/ocellus scale. The 115.2 µm length is the catalog tier, not a published Asterias measurement.",
        "transfer_boundary: The Linckia compound-eye result is not an Asterias rubens wavelength.",
        "transfer_boundary: Tubercle spacing on the unscaled studio plate is dimensionless. A 0.02 arm-radius reference assumes a 5.8 mm arm and is a transfer, not a measurement of this plate." ;
    pk:anatomicalStructure "Turing pigment stripe / ocellus wavelength in Asterias rubens" ;
    pk:aristotelianEfficientCause "1. Chemical Turing / RD" ;
    pk:aristotelianMegistonGenos "II. Anaima (Invertebrata)" ;
    pk:aristotelianPoionForm "Stripes, Spots & Ocelli" ;
    pk:aristotelianPosonLaw "Turing Wavenumber k_c" ;
    pk:aristotelianSubalternClass "Ostrakoderma (Mollusca & Echinodermata)" ;
    pk:biologicalGenus "Asterias" ;
    pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-asterias-rubens-turing-wavelength-s0.py; output: docs/media/asterias_rubens_turing_wavelength_s0_comparison.png; organism: Asterias rubens (common starfish), Holocene, 0 Ma; environment: n/a; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Turing 1952 diffusion-driven instability and a critical wavenumber. | Schnakenberg 1979 two-species reaction with a homogeneous steady state. | Murray 1981 writes the pre-pattern wavenumber used for animal markings. | Garm & Nilsson 2014: most starfish have a compound eye at the arm tip; their navigation experiments used Linckia laevigata.; reconstructed_in_script: a=0.072, b=0.918, D_v/D_u=7.62, domain 576.0 µm, seed 3123, chosen so λ_c = 115.2 µm and only the p=5 mode is unstable. | FTCS Neumann integration, radial-FFT wavelength, diffusivity sweep λ ∝ √s, equal-diffusion ablation. | Segmentation of the Hillewaert photograph (circularity, coherency, tubercle spacing in units of arm radius).; transfer_boundary: Coat-marking reaction-diffusion is transferred to an asteroid pigment/ocellus scale. The 115.2 µm length is the catalog tier, not a published Asterias measurement. | The Linckia compound-eye result is not an Asterias rubens wavelength. | Tubercle spacing on the unscaled studio plate is dimensionless. A 0.02 arm-radius reference assumes a 5.8 mm arm and is a transfer, not a measurement of this plate.; does_not_claim: That 115.2 µm was measured as an ocellus diameter in Asterias rubens. | That the studio photograph is a micrograph at this scale, or that its pixel wavelength is 115.2 µm. | That Schnakenberg rate constants were identified in starfish epidermis. | A second, independent assessor.]; phenomenon_size: 115 µm (0.0001152 m, log10=-3.939 m, tier=5_primordium_100um); species_scale_ladder (10 runs from 115 µm to 36.0 cm): 115 µm:asterias-rubens-bioelectric-polarity, 430 µm:asterias-rubens-vertex-packing, 8.0 mm:asterias-rubens-turing-wavelength, 1.5 cm:asterias-rubens-clock-wavefront, 3.5 cm:asterias-rubens-elastic-buckling, 4.6 cm:asterias-rubens-morphogen-condensation, 6.0 cm:asterias-rubens-flux-canalization, 8.0 cm:asterias-rubens-branching-tree" ;
